# ============================================================================
#  PROMPT RULES (template)  —  read by the /prompt command on every invocation
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  Copy this file to one (or both) of:
#    - .claude/prompt-rules.env          (project rules, this repo only)
#    - ~/.claude/prompt-rules.env        (global rules, every project)
#  Then edit the rules below. /prompt combines both files at runtime.
#
#  FORMAT
#  - One rule per line, written as a plain instruction.
#  - Lines starting with "#" are comments and are ignored. Blank lines ignored.
#
#  PROJECT TAGS (optional)
#  - Rules ABOVE any [project: ...] header apply to EVERY project.
#  - A line like  [project: my-frontend]  scopes the rules under it to that
#    project only (matched by the project's folder / git repo name).
#  - A header may list several:  [project: my-frontend, my-admin]
#  - Use  [project: all]  (or [all]) to return to global scope.
# ============================================================================

# ====================  GLOBAL — applies to every project  ===================

# --- Reuse & DRY (the core rule — follow this exact order every time) -------
For ANYTHING you are about to implement, FIRST search the whole project for an existing version of it (component, class, function, variable, type, constant, style, design token, or util). Do not write a single line of new code until this search is done and its result is reported.
STEP 1 — If a SHARED / reusable version already exists, use it automatically. Never create your own copy of something that is already shared.
STEP 2 — If only a LOCAL or duplicated version exists, promote it to a shared/reusable location and update EVERY existing usage to consume that shared version, so all instances of the project use the same implementation, style, and design.
STEP 3 — Only if nothing exists at all may you create something new, and build it as a shared/reusable piece from the start. Explicitly state that you confirmed nothing reusable existed.
Never leave two implementations of the same thing in the project — one shared version, used everywhere.

# --- Consistency -----------------------------------------------------------
Match the existing code style, naming conventions, folder structure, and patterns of the surrounding code rather than introducing new ones.
Reuse existing design tokens, theme variables, and shared styles instead of hard-coding values.

# --- Safety ----------------------------------------------------------------
Do not break existing callers: when you change a shared piece, verify and update every place that already uses it.
Run the project's existing tests/linters after the change when they are available, and report the result.


# ============  EXAMPLE: project-specific pointer rules  =====================
# Telling /prompt WHERE shared things live is the single most effective rule —
# it turns best-effort reuse into reliable reuse. Replace the name and paths.
#
# [project: my-frontend]
# Shared components live in src/app/shared — always look there FIRST and reuse or extend those before creating anything new.
# When you build a new reusable component, place it under src/app/shared so the whole app can use it, and wire existing duplicates to consume it.

[project: all]
